meta {
  name: Participants are immutable (removed route → 404)
  type: http
  seq: 32
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/sessions/:id/boats
  body: json
  auth: none
}

params:path {
  id: 229
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "deviceId": "{{deviceId}}"
  }
}

docs {
  Todo 55 (2026-10-22): participants are declared ONCE, on POST /sessions, and
  nothing can change them afterwards — not before the gun, not after. This
  request must answer 404: POST /sessions/:id/boats and
  DELETE /sessions/:id/boats/:deviceId no longer exist.

  Why the endpoints went instead of gaining a status guard: status is DERIVED
  on read (only `abandoned` is stored). Adding a boat to a finished race makes
  "every boat uploaded a run" false, so the race silently un-finishes, comes
  back on the health piggyback and can drag the boat into a race that is over.
  A guard fixes that one hole; no route closes the class.

  The frozen table is protected too: GET /sessions/:id/standings re-freezes a
  snapshot whose boats are not the session's boats (it logs when it does), and
  node tools/prune-db.js --standings sweeps the rest in bulk.
}
